\section{Defect probe details}
\label{sup:probe}
This section expands the verification paragraph of Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}.

\textbf{Verification on real local updates.} The decomposition is exact, but it
is cheap to check against the implementation. Starting from one initialisation,
two clients of our CNN\,+\,LSTM run $\dfPropBi$ and $\dfPropBj$ local batches.
Equation~\eqref{eq:cos} reproduces the measured cosine to within
$\dfPropCosErr$ and~\eqref{eq:dist} the squared distance to a relative error of
$\dfPropDistErr$. The full-vector cosine of the two updates is
$\dfPropCosFull$ while their trainable cosine is $\dfPropCosSig$; the counter
alone carries $\dfPropShareI\%$ of the first client's squared norm and
$\dfPropDistShare\%$ of the pair's squared distance.

\textbf{A second architecture.} One BatchNorm layer is the mildest case. We
repeat the check on a CIFAR-style ResNet-18 ($\drNBN$ BatchNorm layers,
$\drNTrain$ trainable coordinates; written out by hand, synthetic
$3\times32\times32$ inputs, SGD with learning rate $0.01$, momentum $0.9$, batch
$32$). The counter is again inert under the default momentum, and the
decomposition again holds to floating-point precision. With $\drPropBi$ and
$\drPropBj$ local batches the counter contributes exactly
$L(B_i-B_j)^2=\drCounterDist$ to the squared distance, which is
$\drPropDistShare\%$ of it; the buffers as a whole carry $\drBufShareI\%$ of the
first client's squared norm (the counter alone $\drPropShareI\%$, the running
statistics most of the rest); and the two clients' transmitted updates have cosine
$\drPropCosFull$ although their trainable updates are nearly orthogonal
($\drPropCosSig$). More BatchNorm layers make the hazard larger, not smaller.

\textbf{Real images, real federated rounds.} FedAvg on CIFAR-10 with a ResNet-20
($\gcNBN$ BatchNorm layers, $\gcNTrain$ trainable and $\gcNBuf$ buffer coordinates,
default momentum): $\gcRows$ images over $\gcClients$ clients, $\gcMal$ flipping
labels ($y\to y+1 \bmod 10$), one local SGD epoch (batch $\gcBS$), a
$\gcRoot$-image FLTrust root set, IID or Dirichlet($0.5$) shards, three seeds,
$\gcRounds$ rounds (\texttt{experiments/generality/cifar\_resnet20.py}; final CA
$\gcAccFedavgDir$ Dir., $\gcAccFedavgIid$ IID). Each round the same client updates
are scored by Krum, Multi-Krum and FLTrust on the full \sd{}, on it without the
counter, and on \texttt{parameters()}, so every difference is a decision
difference on identical inputs (Table~\ref{tab:gen}). On the full vector every
client's cosine to the root update rises and the flippers get their head-count
share of FLTrust's trust instead of being zeroed (Corollary~\ref{cor:fltrust}).
Under IID shards all $B_i$ are equal, so the counter cancels from every distance
and Krum's remaining differences come from the running statistics. In closed loop
(Dir., $n=\gcFtN$ per arm) the gap persists: FLTrust driven by the full vector gives
the flippers $\gcFtLoopFull\%$ of its trust against $\gcFtLoopTr\%$, Multi-Krum
keeps $\gcMkLoopFull$ against $\gcMkLoopTr$ of them per round. Final accuracy does
not separate the arms: FLTrust $\gcFtAccFull$ against $\gcFtAccTr$ (near chance in
both; a $\gcRoot$-image root barely trains in $\gcRounds$ rounds), Multi-Krum
$\gcMkAccFull$ against $\gcMkAccTr$. Two flippers of ten are a weak attack: the
decisions move; accuracy, here, does not.

