\textbf{A seed-robust adversary.} Calibrating on one seed was a mistake, and the
campaign exposed it. Under example-weighted aggregation an attacker's weight is its
data share. With two clients drawn at random from a Dirichlet($0.5$) partition,
that share ranges from $\csShareRandMin$ to $\csShareRandMax$ over seeds $42$--$51$,
against $20\%$ on every IID seed. At $E_m=5$, FedAvg implants the backdoor on the
two seeds among $42$--$46$ where the attackers hold the most data, and on the other
three the attacked BSR stays at the clean floor (Table~\ref{tab:seedcal}). In the
campaign reported below, attack strength was therefore a property of the partition
draw, not of the threat model, and its non-IID attacked BSR columns are
uninformative on those seeds. The re-run fixes the adversary's budget instead. It
corrupts two clients whose combined data share is within $\csShareTol$ percentage
point of $m$ (\texttt{attack.mal\_selection: share\_matched}; the random draw is
kept when it already qualifies, otherwise a pair is drawn uniformly among those
that do). The partition and every honest shard are unchanged, and the attackers'
share becomes $\csShareMatMin$--$\csShareMatMax$ ($\csShareNchanged$ of
$\csShareNseeds$ non-IID seeds change attackers). Matching the share is not enough.
At $E_m=5$ the backdoor fails to implant on seeds $44$, $45$ and $46$; on seed
$45$ the random attackers, holding $\csShareRandFortyFive$ of the data, had
implanted it (BSR $\csRandBSRFortyFive$), and the matched pair, holding
$\csShareMatFortyFive$, does not ($\csSmEfiveBSRFortyFive$). More local
work rescues seed $44$ (BSR $\csSmEtenBSRFortyFour$ at $E_m=10$,
$\csSmEfifteenBSRFortyFour$ at $E_m=15$) but not seed $46$ ($\csSmEtenBSRFortySix$ and
$\csSmEfifteenBSRFortySix$, with single rounds above $0.5$ that the next round
undoes). No single $E_m$ we tried makes the attack implant on every seed.

What seed $46$ does differently is, as far as we can tell, timing. Where the
backdoor implants it does so abruptly: BSR sits at the clean floor and then jumps
past $0.5$ within one round, at round $\csOnsetMin$--$\csOnsetMax$ in the
$\csOnsetN$ implanting cells of Table~\ref{tab:seedcal}. On seed $46$ the jump
comes at round $\csSmEtenImplantFortySix$ ($E_m=10$) or
$\csSmEfifteenImplantFortySix$ ($E_m=15$), too late or too briefly to register
under a last-five-round criterion. Our reading is
that the attack sits near a threshold on seed $46$, not that the seed is immune.
The poisoned data do not single the seed out
(\texttt{experiments/calib\_iter6/s46\_probe.py}): the attackers stamp
$\csPoisRowsMin$--$\csPoisRowsMax$ rows on every seed, DDoS is the majority source
class everywhere, and $\csTrigNoutRange$ of the $\csTrigNcols$ stamped values fall
outside the training range. Seed $46$'s attackers hold few genuine benign rows
($\csMalBenignFortySix$, against $\csMalBenignImplMin$--$\csMalBenignImplMax$ on
seeds $42$--$44$); seeds $49$ and $51$ ($\csMalBenignFortyNine$ and
$\csMalBenignFiftyOne$), registered as the test before their cells finished, both
implant strongly at $E_m=15$ (BSR $\csSmEfifteenBSRFortyNine$ and
$\csSmEfifteenBSRFiftyOne$), so benign
scarcity does not explain seed $46$.
A $40$-round re-run tests the censoring reading. Seed $44$ at $E_m=5$, which never
implants within $15$ rounds, implants at round $\csHzImplantFortyFourEfive$ of
$\csHzRoundsFortyFourEfive$ (BSR last at or below $0.5$ at round
$\csHzLastLowFortyFourEfive$, last-five mean $\csHzBSRFortyFourEfive$): on that one
cell the $15$-round failure was a matter of time. So was seed $46$'s at $E_m=15$:
its BSR first exceeds $0.5$ at round $\csHzImplantFortySixEfifteen$ of
$\csHzRoundsFortySixEfifteen$, falls back, and stays above it after round
$\csHzLastLowFortySixEfifteen$ (last-five mean $\csHzBSRFortySixEfifteen$). Two cells, one each, support the threshold reading;
they do not change the $15$-round campaign, whose implant set is decided by its own
FedAvg cells (Sec.~\ref{sec:p1}).

The rule for the re-run was fixed before the ten calibration cells finished: use
$E_m=15$ if FedAvg implants (last-five-round BSR above $0.5$) on at least eight of
the ten non-IID seeds. It implants on $\csSmEfifteenNimpl$ of ten; seed $46$ is
the exception, and seed $45$ only just clears the bar ($\csSmEfifteenBSRFortyFive$,
first round above $0.5$ at round $\csSmEfifteenImplantFortyFive$). A rule's
attacked BSR is read only on seeds where the re-run's own FedAvg cell implants,
and the excluded seeds are reported with the results. The re-run executes on a GPU
and the calibration ran on a CPU, so the two are reproductions of each other, not
bit-identical copies. On the first seed both have finished (seed $43$), the re-run's
FedAvg cell implants as the calibration did (BSR $\csRepBSRFortyThree$, first round
above $0.5$ at round $\csRepImplantFortyThree$, against $\csSmEfifteenBSRFortyThree$
at round $\csSmEfifteenImplantFortyThree$).

